Mimoun"] " (FRA/ALG), " [:a {:href "/athletes/70477"} "Herbert Schade"] " (FRG), " [:a {:href "/athletes/69394"} "Gordon Pirie"] " (GBR), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/68952"} "Chris Chataway"] " (GBR). Pirie was broken at about 4,500 metres, with the other four reaching the bell lap together. Chataway then took the lead, followed by Schade and Mimoun. Zátopek made a furious rush on the final curve and took the lead back. Chataway was wasted and stepped on the curve coming to the home stretch and fell. He would get up and finish fifth, passed also by Pirie. Zátopek was not challenged on the straight, with Mimoun holding on for the silver medal ahead of Schade."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Zátopek’s wife, " [:a {:href "/athletes/76547"} "Dana Zátopkova"] ", won the " [:a {:href "/results/59232"} "women’s javelin"] " gold medal later in the afternoon. Zátopek himself would return a few days later to win the " [:a {:href "/results/58937"} "marathon"] ", completing an unmatched Olympic distance triple. Chataway is also well known as one of the two pacers (with " [:a {:href "/athletes/68910"} "Chris Brasher"] ") who helped " [:a {:href "/athletes/68857"} "Roger Bannister"] " break the 4-minute mile barrier in 1954. He would also set a world record in the 5,000 in 1954. Mimoun’s silver was the third time he had finished second to Zátopek in an Olympic distance final – " [:a {:href "/results/58937"} "1948 10K"] ", and both 1952 5K and 10K. He would return to win the " [:a {:href "/results/59330"} "marathon in 1956 at Melbourne"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "903"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/76736"} "Emil Zátopek"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-